WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.027
RIGHT JOIN is the mirror image of LEFT JOIN.

00:00:03.027 --> 00:00:08.642
Instead of preserving every row from the left side, it preserves every row from the right side.

00:00:08.642 --> 00:00:12.512
In the tiny example, the admissions table is on the right.

00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:18.727
Admission 104 belongs to patient ID 4, which has no matching patient row.

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:24.072
Because the right side is preserved, admission 104 remains in the result and the patient columns become NULL.

00:00:24.072 --> 00:00:28.322
The runnable example uses a different real-world relationship.

00:00:28.322 --> 00:00:32.622
We join medical staff to departments and preserve every department.

00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:40.282
If a department has no staff member, the department still appears and the staff columns become NULL.

00:00:40.282 --> 00:00:46.587
RIGHT JOIN is valid PostgreSQL syntax, but many teams prefer to rewrite it as a LEFT JOIN by swapping the

00:00:46.587 --> 00:00:48.242
table order.

00:00:48.242 --> 00:00:52.047
That often makes the preserved population easier to see because the table you want to keep appears first.

00:00:52.047 --> 00:00:58.042
So the important lesson is not that RIGHT JOIN is bad.

00:00:58.042 --> 00:01:00.887
The lesson is to make preservation obvious.

00:01:00.887 --> 00:01:06.572
If your question says every department, structure the query so another reader can quickly see that departments

00:01:06.572 --> 00:01:08.836
are the population being preserved.
